      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be formed from the letters in \"EQUALS\" with the constraints that each sequence must begin with \"L\" and end with \"Q\", and no letter can appear more than once.\n\nFirst, identify the letters available for the sequence: E, Q, U, A, L, S.\n\nSince the sequence must start with \"L\" and end with \"Q\", the sequence will be in the form \"L _ _ Q\". We need to fill the two middle positions with two of the remaining letters E, U, A, and S. The letters E, U, A, and S are 4 in total, and we need to choose 2 out of these 4 letters to fill the middle positions.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 2 letters out of 4 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) elements from a set of \\( n \\) elements without regard to the order of selection. Here, \\( n = 4 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{4}{2} = \\frac{4!}{2!2!} = \\frac{4 \\times 3}{2 \\times 1} = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 6 ways to choose the two letters for the middle positions. For each choice of these two letters, there is only one way to arrange them in the middle positions (i.e., the sequence is fixed with \"L\" at the beginning and \"Q\" at the end).\n\nTherefore, the total number of distinct sequences is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
